# UST.G3CN: Pole-Six Family Transform First-Jet Theorem

## Result

The frozen 225-generator original-Jacobian transform has now been differentiated
along the selected G3BI affine family on every one of its thirty Cox-degree
charts.  The family changes five of the ten original Jacobian generators:

```text
dPhi/dx, dPhi/dy, dPhi/dz, dPhi/dt0, dPhi/ds.
```

For each chart the selected square augmented matrix has the exact form

```text
C_delta(a) = C_delta(0) + a U_delta E_delta^T.
```

The thirty base matrices were already certified invertible.  The family update
uses 287,275 selected image columns in total, and the largest Woodbury core has
dimension at most 16,143.  The exact number-field updates contain 4,630,265
nonzero entries; 4,415,714 survive at `(11,gamma-3)`.

## Decisive first-order test

For every selected generator coordinate matrix `X_delta(a)`, differentiation
at B89 gives

```text
C_delta(0) X_delta'(0)
  = -U_delta E_delta^T X_delta(0).
```

All thirty systems were solved with the existing certified factors and replayed
independently with zero residual.  Twenty-eight groups have nonzero quotient
coordinates, with 15,211 nonzero quotient entries in total.  Therefore the
frozen transform cannot be promoted along the family merely by varying its ten
coefficient rows.  Its induced quotient defect must be carried into the common
pole-six target calculation.

This is not a pole-six source obstruction.  It is a precise rejection of one
too-small promotion rule.  UST.G3CO addresses the common target degree.

